## Error Recovery Patterns

### Graceful Degradation
- Continue operation when non-critical components fail
- Implement fallback mechanisms
- Log errors but don't stop the entire system

### Retry Logic
```go
// Implement retry with exponential backoff
maxRetries := 3
for i := 0; i < maxRetries; i++ {
    err := operation()
    if err == nil {
        break
    }
    if i == maxRetries-1 {
        logger.ErrorFmt("Operation failed after %d retries: %v", maxRetries, err)
        return err
    }
    time.Sleep(time.Duration(i+1) * time.Second)
}
```

### Circuit Breaker Pattern
- Implement cooldown periods for failing operations
- Use triangle locking to prevent rapid re-execution
- Monitor error rates and adjust behavior accordingly

## Log File Management
- Use lumberjack for log rotation
- Separate log files for different components
- Implement log level filtering
- Archive old logs automatically

## Performance Considerations
- Use structured logging to avoid string concatenation
- Implement log sampling for high-frequency events
- Use appropriate log levels to control verbosity
- Consider async logging for high-throughput scenarios

## Testing and Debugging
- Use consistent log formats for easier parsing
- Include correlation IDs for tracing requests
- Implement debug modes for detailed logging
- Use log analysis tools for production monitoring